WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.651 --> 00:00:13.410
And today's feature
presenter is Bill Burnett.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.310
Bill is an Adjunct Professor
of Mechanical Engineering and

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:20.350
the Executive Director of the design
program at Stanford University.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.590
He directs undergraduates and
graduate programs in design and

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.710
teaches at the d school.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.620
Bill received his bachelors of science and
master of science in

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.760
product design at Stanford, and has worked
in startups and Fortune 100 companies.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.320
Including seven years at Apple
designing award winning laptops, and

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.110
a number of years in the toy industry
designing Star Wars action figures.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:46.030
He holds a number of mechanical and
design patents and design awards.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.750
And in addition to his duties at Stanford,
he is on the board of Voz,

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:54.340
a socially responsible fashion startup and
advises several other startup companies.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.150
And now I'd like to turn
the floor over to Bill.

00:00:57.643 --> 00:00:58.249
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:00:58.249 --> 00:01:00.577
Let's see.

00:01:00.577 --> 00:01:03.440
There we go.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:04.740
There's the first five.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:08.370
So today, we're going to talk about
this idea we called designing your life,

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:13.020
which is sort of an interesting
sort of grand claim.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.753
Can people actually use design
thinking to design their lives?

00:01:16.753 --> 00:01:19.958
For those of you who are familiar,
I'm sure many of you

00:01:19.958 --> 00:01:24.557
are with the design thinking ideas,
the diagram that we have for our process,

00:01:24.557 --> 00:01:28.442
says design thinking process
starts with empathy for users.

00:01:28.442 --> 00:01:33.570
They would trying to come up with a new
kind of mobile phone, or a new application

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.990
for dieting or something we talk to users,
we figure out what they really need.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:39.330
We typically redefine and

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:43.080
reframe the problem a bunch of times
until we're sure we've got it right.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.010
Come up with lots of ideas in the
idealization phase, and then we build and

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:47.860
test, prototype and test our way forward,

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.140
and that's just the way
design thinking works.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.720
It's a very iterative and
generative process.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.620
Companies like IBM, and Apple, and

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.830
other people are using this
process to innovate products.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.520
So the kind of the question becomes, gee,

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:09.220
this is a human centered problem solving
approach, can we apply design thinking

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:14.280
to this wicked problem, of designing maybe
your job, or redesigning your career, and

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:20.200
potentially even adjusting the issue of,
how do I have a meaningful life?

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:25.100
And so about ten years ago,
my co-founder of the Design Your Life Lab,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:31.140
the DY Life Lab at Stanford Dave Evans and
I started a conversation about this idea.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.830
Could you apply design thinking
to the wicked problem?

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.610
Wicked problem is a technical term
is problems that are open ended, and

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:41.120
they're hard to solve, and as soon as
you solve them, new problems emerge.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.896
Could use design thinking to work on
things like having a meaningful life?

00:02:45.896 --> 00:02:49.840
And it really gets back to the question
we get asked in office hours, and

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.931
we get asked when we're doing
workshops and seminars,

00:02:52.931 --> 00:02:57.008
it's the one question that doesn't
even seem to go away and that's the,

00:02:57.008 --> 00:02:59.335
what do I want to be
when I grow up question?

00:02:59.335 --> 00:03:00.409
Now if we were alive,

00:03:00.409 --> 00:03:04.378
I'd ask you to all raise your hands if
you've ever been asked this question.

00:03:04.378 --> 00:03:07.065
I think 100% of all humans on the planet,

00:03:07.065 --> 00:03:11.734
in every culture I've done this workshop
in Portugal and Spain, and Italy and

00:03:11.734 --> 00:03:16.684
in Taiwan, and Korea, and China, everybody
says, yeah, people always ask me,

00:03:16.684 --> 00:03:21.030
what I want to do when I grow up, and
sometimes I ask myself that question.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:25.011
So here's our first reframe in design
thinking is big on coming up with the new

00:03:25.011 --> 00:03:28.600
way of looking at the problem,
we call it reframe.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.360
Are we going to ever want you to grow up?

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:34.260
At least if growing up means losing that
childlike curiosity about that world,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.080
that hunger to learn new things.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.590
So we refrain this problem as,

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:43.300
what do I want to grow into,
as I explore the rest of my life?

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.730
Now, we've been doing dual workshops
based on the book that came out

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.880
in September all over the country.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.678
And, we have yet to meet anyone.

00:03:50.678 --> 00:03:55.864
In the very first workshop we did for the
Stanford Alumni Association in New York,

00:03:55.864 --> 00:03:59.939
we had a woman sitting front and
center in a room of 350 alums.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:04.900
She was from the class of 1950,
she was 80-something years old.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.050
And she thought the rest of her life
was going to be very, very interesting.

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:11.767
So we have never met anybody who doesn't
think that it would be worth having

00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:15.897
some tools and ideas about how to explore
this growing into the rest of their lives.

00:04:15.897 --> 00:04:19.230
So that's our reframe, and
we've been teaching this

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:24.130
class at Stanford since 2006, so
a little more than ten years.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.720
And we started out with just
a class called Designing Your Life.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.440
So we started out with a class for just
the kids in my major, the design students,

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:32.640
called The Designer's Voice.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.260
But that very quickly became a general
class for everyone at the university.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.554
Engineers, non-engineers,
science majors, history majors,

00:04:40.554 --> 00:04:43.904
people from the Drama Department,
people from the Art Department,

00:04:43.904 --> 00:04:47.156
people from computer science,
all the students trust the board.

00:04:47.156 --> 00:04:49.644
And we have a class for
seniors called Designing Your Life,

00:04:49.644 --> 00:04:52.550
because they're the ones with they're
kind of of the biggest pain point.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.010
They're about to launch into the world,
and they've never been anything but

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:56.808
a student before.

00:04:56.808 --> 00:04:59.440
Then we were asked to do a class for
Stanford Freshmen, so

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.960
we have a Designing Your Stanford for
freshmen.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.196
We say don't do Stanford
like you did high school.

00:05:04.196 --> 00:05:06.590
It's a much bigger experience.

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:10.590
And you don't want to be so ballistic,
you don't want to just pick a major and

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:15.340
go because that's not utilizing
Stanford for all the resources it has.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.370
And we also do a class for designing
professional for master students and

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:22.810
PhD students, who are thinking about,
do they want to go into academics or

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:24.800
do they want to do a career in industry.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.100
And the same ideas and tools apply to all
of these people accept slightly different

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.410
framing questions because there
are different stages in their life.

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:37.764
We also do this off campus with career
professionals, and we just the director of

00:05:37.764 --> 00:05:42.964
our lab the managing director of our lab
Kathy Davis teaches Designing Your Life

00:05:42.964 --> 00:05:47.561
for Women weekend down at the Asilomar
down at the coast here in the Bay Area.

00:05:47.561 --> 00:05:52.694
So there's lots and lots of interesting
stuff, and we actually measured

00:05:52.694 --> 00:05:58.680
the results around whether or not taking
the class had an impact on people.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:03.807
And I dug out this old poster, this is
a very busy slide, I apologize, but

00:06:03.807 --> 00:06:09.050
it's normally a big sort of 3 foot by
2 foot poster given at a conference.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.667
And this was the work of Lindsay Oishi,

00:06:11.667 --> 00:06:15.160
who was a PhD candidate
studying the class.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:18.363
And one of the things she did was she
studied people who took the class,

00:06:18.363 --> 00:06:20.870
just a random group of students
who were her controls.

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:25.368
And a group of students who wanted to
take the class but couldn't get in,

00:06:25.368 --> 00:06:30.414
who just had intention, but didn't have
the actual intervention of the class.

00:06:30.414 --> 00:06:34.986
The class outcomes were, you're looking
at that little yellow box in the middle,

00:06:34.986 --> 00:06:38.920
the confidence to explore careers and
make good decisions went way up.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.421
The belief in these career myths,
what I'll call dysfunctional beliefs, and

00:06:41.421 --> 00:06:43.040
I'll talk about those in a second,
went way down.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:48.990
And the ability to identify and achieve
specific occupational goals went way up.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:53.120
And, if that little chart on the far
right there, the red, green and

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:55.460
blue bars, is an indication.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.973
People grew in their flexibility
to have new ideas, and

00:06:58.973 --> 00:07:02.420
in their creativity to
imagine new possible futures.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:06.580
So it's pretty clear that if you just
take the class, or in this case,

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.893
maybe read the book that you
can have a higher confidence

00:07:09.893 --> 00:07:13.440
that you can choose well as you
move forward in your career.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.259
That you will be more inventive and
creative,

00:07:16.259 --> 00:07:20.180
if you practice the techniques
taught in the class.

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:25.660
It was really nice to hear that on a
scientific basis with a controlled study,

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:29.420
we could have that kind of
an impact on a student's outcome.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:32.810
Part of that was we get rid of what
psychologists call dysfunctional beliefs.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:37.210
The first one we try to blow up is this
belief that the organizing question for

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:38.240
your life is, what's your passion?

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:40.580
Again if we were alive I'd say,

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:43.520
how many people have been asked this
question in the last month or two?

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.965
And experiences any evidence,

00:07:45.965 --> 00:07:49.950
90% of you would say, yeah,
this question comes up all the time.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:52.457
It's a terrible question we think for

00:07:52.457 --> 00:07:55.519
a couple of reasons and
here's the data on it.

00:07:55.519 --> 00:07:58.467
Now I'm not going to say that,
if you knew at age five,

00:07:58.467 --> 00:08:03.075
you wanted to be a ballerina and you
are now dancing with the New York Company.

00:08:03.075 --> 00:08:07.450
Awesome that you've found your passion and
you tracked to it and you achieved it.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:12.020
But the data says that only 20%
of the population can actually

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:13.420
answer this question.

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:15.750
Bill Damon wrote a fantastic
book Path to Purpose.

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:19.120
Bill is the head of the center for the
study of adolescence here at Stanford and

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:23.960
a top researcher in the field of careers,
purpose, meaning.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:28.850
They did a pretty extensive study,
and very few people, one out of five,

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:32.460
can say I know what I want to do and
I'm kind of going for it.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:36.750
Everybody else either has many
things that they're interested in or

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.680
no one thing that rises to
whatever the level of passion is.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:42.900
So where I'm uncomfortable with
the technique that says okay,

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:44.940
you come to the front of the line and
I say what's your passion?

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:45.810
You say, I don't know.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:46.850
And I say then go to the back of the line.

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.700
When you figure it out,
come back and I'll work with you.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:52.731
That just doesn't seem fair.

00:08:52.731 --> 00:08:53.904
People come to us all the time and

00:08:53.904 --> 00:08:57.070
say you know, I should've identified
a passion by now, but I don't have one.

00:08:57.070 --> 00:08:58.710
And they feel like they've
done something wrong.

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:02.510
And the data is,
you're not wrong, you're normal.

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:06.300
So don't worry about this question.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:09.830
Cal Newport wrote a book called So
Good They Can't Ignore You, or

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:11.420
something to that effect.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:13.200
And in which he tracked this same thing.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.090
And he says look, passion is the outcome
of working hard in a field that you love

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:19.660
and discovering that it
is truly your calling.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:22.110
It's an end point,
it's not a starting point.

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:25.340
So if you can take one thing
away from the talk today,

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:28.990
the law of this belief that you
have to have a passion is not true.

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:33.770
Second, dysfunctional belief is you
should know where you're going by now.

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:37.860
And if you're not, you're late and
eye of an eye **** up.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:41.091
That was 25,
by 25 you're supposed to have sort of

00:09:41.091 --> 00:09:44.057
primary relationship in the oven and
ready to go.

00:09:44.057 --> 00:09:46.358
And you were supposed to have a job that
was the thing that you were going to do

00:09:46.358 --> 00:09:47.204
for the rest of your life.

00:09:47.204 --> 00:09:50.070
And if you didn't have it by 25,
you were late.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:51.430
There was something wrong with you.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:56.110
Now my students would probably say 30, but
the point is, it's a ridiculous question.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:57.870
There's going to be more than
one of you in there anyway.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:09:59.380
You're going to have multiple careers.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:02.440
You can't be late because you haven't,
late for which career?

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:02.950
The second one?

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:04.380
The third one?

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:08.410
The first one is often
exploratory in nature, and so

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:11.430
the fact that you're trying lots
of things is the new normal.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:14.406
There's no such thing as being late.

00:10:14.406 --> 00:10:19.178
The third one, which probably is
the most dysfunctional one is that

00:10:19.178 --> 00:10:23.040
you should be optimizing
the very best version of you.

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:27.220
There is one singular version of
you implied in this question and

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:28.110
you should find it.

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:32.890
And if you don't find it, then you are not
actually having the best possible life and

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:34.977
you are settling for
something less than that.

00:10:34.977 --> 00:10:38.600
And I ran through this with
lots of people that I talk

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:42.320
to in our little mini workshops, and
they're like, you know I picked something,

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:47.130
but it's really not what I wanted, but
I can't change and it's not the best.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:49.910
And we're like well, one, you can change.

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:50.930
Two, it's never too late.

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:55.290
And three, this idea of best
implies some singular path.

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:59.300
I mean look, all of us,
if we looked backwards in our lives and

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:02.280
I asked you to honestly tell me,
how did you get here?

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:05.400
How did you get to the thing
you're doing today?

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:07.900
You would have to argue
that some of it was choice.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:09.550
I made some good choices along the way.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:13.850
Some of it was, hey some opportunities
showed up, I put myself in a context where

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:16.902
those opportunities might happen,
and that was great.

00:11:16.902 --> 00:11:18.690
And a bunch of it is luck.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:20.640
I'll tell you my story.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.870
I ended up a professor at Stanford.

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:24.460
I never planned to do that.

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:27.450
I grew up in the East Coast in Boston,
was just back there recently,

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:32.150
and it reminded me that everybody thought
I was going to go to Harvard or Yale and

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:36.450
when the letter for Stanford came, and I
didn't even know what Stanford really was.

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:41.780
This was a long time ago before
the Internet and famous colleges.

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:43.430
The letter from Stanford came and
I picked it so

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:46.460
I could get as far away from my parents
as possible when I went to college.

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:47.860
That was my sole criteria.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:49.760
I came here as a physics major.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:52.665
I washed out of physics in about
two quarters and then decided I'd

00:11:52.665 --> 00:11:55.905
invent my own major, physics and
art, because I was always an artist.

00:11:55.905 --> 00:11:59.375
When I went to declare that major,
I discovered that this campus has the one

00:11:59.375 --> 00:12:03.545
singular program in the entire
academic world called product design,

00:12:03.545 --> 00:12:08.332
which combines physics, art,
psychology, anthropology and

00:12:08.332 --> 00:12:10.492
a bunch of other things that
I was deeply interested in.

00:12:10.492 --> 00:12:12.482
It was dumb luck that I ended here.

00:12:12.482 --> 00:12:14.172
Had I gone to Harvard I'd
have been a lawyer.

00:12:14.172 --> 00:12:15.162
I always wanted to be a lawyer.

00:12:15.162 --> 00:12:16.402
I never wanted to be an engineer.

00:12:18.132 --> 00:12:19.892
That said, I made some good choices and

00:12:19.892 --> 00:12:23.132
I put myself in the context where
those choices could get realized.

00:12:23.132 --> 00:12:24.422
There's no one best version.

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:28.260
There's an old expression in business,
good is the enemy of better,

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:30.220
better's the enemy of best.

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:32.348
You should always try to do your best.

00:12:32.348 --> 00:12:33.783
Dave reframes that as,

00:12:33.783 --> 00:12:38.030
the unavailable best is the enemy
of all the available betters.

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:42.750
There's so many other ways that you can
experience a life that's meaningful and

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:45.920
a career that is moving in
the direction that makes sense to you,

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:47.310
that has a purpose.

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:51.010
But getting rid of this notion that if you
don't have the right one or the best one

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:56.850
or the only one, that you're somehow
settling, which is a terrible feeling.

00:12:56.850 --> 00:13:01.930
Getting rid of this notion tends to
release people from a lot of inaction and

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:04.770
start them towards doing stuff.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:11.200
In the class we kind of support the design
thinking model with two things.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.240
We talk about the meaning making layer.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:16.170
In all the research we
did to create the class.

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:19.230
And we did a lot of research in
positive psychology literature and

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:24.710
general literature of healthy people and
what makes healthy people happy,

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:29.210
and also the literature and
research on the students themselves.

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:30.790
I did lots of me finding.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:33.590
Everybody said,
the goal of this experience

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:34.930
of life is to do something meaningful.

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:36.580
I want my life to have a purpose.

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.690
I want to know what it was for.

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:41.982
So we have a meaning making layer we call
the Point of View, the Workview, and

00:13:41.982 --> 00:13:44.887
the Worldview, and have all these
assignments around that layer.

00:13:44.887 --> 00:13:50.757
And then we have what we call,
The design thinking piece or

00:13:50.757 --> 00:13:56.260
and mind mapping, and re-framing,
and all the mindsets of a designer.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:14:00.930
And then we have the Discovery &amp; Support
layer where we talk about the practices

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:03.610
you have to have so
that you are ready to make good decisions.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:04.750
How do you discern things?

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:05.920
How do you know and

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:09.720
make decisions about things with
something other than just logic.

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:13.710
Emotional intelligence, kinesthetic
intelligence, other ways of knowing.

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:15.290
We talk about the importance
of mentors and

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:17.220
community because you
can't do this by yourself.

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:21.790
We encourage people to work through
the book in design teams and

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:26.780
we're tracking over 400 teams now that are
doing that as like a book or design club.

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:28.540
You have to do this together.

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:33.110
So this is kind of the visual syllabus,
if you will, of the class.

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:34.810
I don't have time to do
all of these things, but

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:39.530
I want to touch on three that I
think are kind of fun and important.

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:41.190
One is this idea of flow.

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:43.537
A guy named Mihalyi Csziksentmihalyi,

00:14:43.537 --> 00:14:48.712
who's got the hardest to pronounce name in
psychology, colleague of Martin Seligman,

00:14:48.712 --> 00:14:52.450
wrote a book called Flow
the Psychology of Optimal Experience.

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:53.950
Now you've all been in states of flow.

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:55.197
You might call it something different.

00:14:55.197 --> 00:14:57.810
The zone, if you were an athlete.

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:01.237
Or, just in the moment if
you like that framing.

00:15:01.237 --> 00:15:05.317
But it's a thing where you're doing
something, you're working on something,

00:15:05.317 --> 00:15:06.420
time stands still.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:08.730
You're completely involved in the thing.

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:10.560
You have a sense of energy.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:12.260
He calls it a sense of ecstasy about that.

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:16.940
There's some inner clarity about
the purpose of the thing you're doing.

00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:22.180
And people experience this sort
of a timeless, serene moment.

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:23.800
Pretty high, high thing.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:28.630
He has a model that he calls
the flow zone, or the flow channel,

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:33.610
where the challenge that

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:38.370
you're facing is tough enough, so
that it's really challenging your skills.

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:44.580
But it's not so challenging that you've
moved over into anxiety or distress.

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:46.570
Like this is too hard,
I'm not doing it well.

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:50.210
And it's not so easy that you're
getting bored of the task.

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:55.022
So you're allowed to hyper-focus
in pushing your skill just to

00:15:55.022 --> 00:15:59.134
the edge of the limit of
the tasks you're re doing now.

00:15:59.134 --> 00:16:03.486
Athletes will do that in a moment in
athletics where they just feel like

00:16:03.486 --> 00:16:06.069
they're totally connected to the team or

00:16:06.069 --> 00:16:09.267
to the moment, or
knew where the ball was going to be.

00:16:09.267 --> 00:16:12.135
Scientist experience this by
just going into the lab and

00:16:12.135 --> 00:16:16.407
writing things on a white board, and just
kind of not even exhausting themselves,

00:16:16.407 --> 00:16:20.660
which is just being completely
inside the problem solving moment.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:22.750
I have moments of this when I'm teaching,

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:27.320
I have moments of this during
conversations in office hours.

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.250
But it can also have these
very simple moments.

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:32.590
I like to cook and
I'm sort of chopping onions,

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:35.730
doing my Mise-en-place for the meal.

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:37.810
Getting everything prepped and ready.

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:41.390
That's a pro moment for me, because
I feel competent in the kitchen and

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:46.520
I'm trying new things, and
I'm wanting to make a wonderful meal.

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:52.630
So our idea about this is that
you should be finding yourself in

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:57.562
your career and in your life,
in moments of flow every week.

00:16:59.559 --> 00:17:02.386
If you're not finding that to happen,

00:17:02.386 --> 00:17:08.060
there's one of two things that
are possible from the literature and.

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:09.997
One is you're not noticing.

00:17:09.997 --> 00:17:12.878
A lot of our lives were so
busy now and we're doing so

00:17:12.878 --> 00:17:15.100
much that we just don't notice stuff.

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:21.640
And so keeping a journal,
which is one of the principal practices we

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:26.190
teach our students, is a good way of
just bringing things to your attention.

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:28.220
Now there's two ways of noticing.

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:29.730
One way, which is being in the moment.

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:31.840
There's a lot of mindfulness
stuff in the world right now.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:33.442
I think that's kind of cool.

00:17:33.442 --> 00:17:35.710
And then the other way of
noticing is in retrospect.

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:38.640
Going back at the end of your day and
journaling about,

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:41.150
was there any time where
I felt a sense of flow or

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:44.870
even a preliminary flow
moment maybe was possible?

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:49.830
Are there times during the day where
things were working for me very well and

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:50.895
things that weren't working?

00:17:50.895 --> 00:17:54.930
And by keeping a journal,
what the psychologist noticed is once

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:58.680
you start noticing,
taking the time to notice in retrospect.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:03.560
Certain behaviors or certain moods that
you're in, you will reinforce your ability

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:08.145
to one, notice those moods and
they will happen more frequently.

00:18:08.145 --> 00:18:12.870
And there's an old saying work
isn't supposed to be fun,

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:16.180
that's why they call it work,
we totally disagree.

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:21.420
Since work is the thing you're going
to be doing 40 or 50 hours a week.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:25.100
In addition to everything else you do,
it's one of the dominant activities and

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:27.340
behaviors of your day and your week.

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:29.170
And so you should be enjoying it.

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:32.310
Now there's some startling
statistics around this.

00:18:32.310 --> 00:18:35.650
Gallup does a poll every couple years and

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:40.310
something like 70% of people say they're
deeply disengaged from the work they do.

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:42.460
And another 15% say
they're just disengaged.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:46.813
So we'll say 85% of American workers,
get up on Monday morning and say,

00:18:46.813 --> 00:18:48.319
I don't want to go to work.

00:18:48.319 --> 00:18:49.151
It doesn't mean anything to me.

00:18:49.151 --> 00:18:54.290
This is startling in dysfunction.

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:58.380
You don't want to be in that 85%,
so one of the ways to notice that

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:02.380
the work you're doing has some meaning
is that it creates these flow states.

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:05.410
So a flow journal is our
first recommendation.

00:19:07.349 --> 00:19:11.733
The other thing about flow is it's

00:19:11.733 --> 00:19:16.125
about where your energy is going.

00:19:19.532 --> 00:19:23.844
To a large extent, our experience of
the world is sort of what's going on in

00:19:23.844 --> 00:19:28.280
our head, that part of your head
that's always talking to you.

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:31.240
There's a part of you head that's talking
to you saying, hey this is a really

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:35.000
fun experience, or hey I'm really learning
a lot, or this is really interesting.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:39.110
Then you're experiencing your day or
that moment as meaningful.

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:42.550
If the talking in your head is,
I don't know what I'm doing this for,

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:45.330
I have no context for this,
this isn't interesting.

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:46.560
Then that's your reality.

00:19:47.590 --> 00:19:52.150
And so we developed a little tool that's
been pretty successful with students and

00:19:52.150 --> 00:19:54.460
with mid-career people and everybody.

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:56.470
A lot of people talking
about time management.

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:59.240
If I just managed my week better,

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:02.880
my time management just did the important
stuff not the urgent stuff.

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:07.820
All that stuff's fine, but
what we find is it's a little cumbersome

00:20:07.820 --> 00:20:11.684
to keep track of, time and
time management systems.

00:20:11.684 --> 00:20:16.700
And really what we thing you're actually
trying to keep track of is not so

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:22.180
much how many hours did I spend on task
A or B, but how do I feel about it?

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:26.700
And so we reframed the time management
thing to energy management.

00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:32.026
So the way you do this, you take some
things from your flow journal for

00:20:32.026 --> 00:20:35.307
the week, or just take your Google
calendar, whatever you've got.

00:20:35.307 --> 00:20:38.967
Take it out, write down all of
the repetitive meetings, activities,

00:20:38.967 --> 00:20:40.920
whatever that you do during the week.

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:44.930
And that's just work things, like I got
the budget meeting, I got this I got that.

00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:48.820
But I take my kids to soccer games, and I
coach a little league team on the weekend.

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:49.831
Any of your regular engagements.

00:20:49.831 --> 00:20:54.972
And then chart those engagements
each specific engagement and

00:20:54.972 --> 00:20:59.825
put them in order of beginning
of the week, end of the week.

00:20:59.825 --> 00:21:04.720
And chart them in terms of how
energetic are those activities?

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:09.290
And I don't mean this in a sort of
California new age energy thing.

00:21:09.290 --> 00:21:13.690
I'm just saying there's some stuff you
do and when you do it you're done.

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:16.680
You're just as energetic or
more energetic when you started.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:20.480
It kind of feeds you it gives you energy,
feels purposeful.

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:24.550
And there's other stuff you do and
you leave the task and you're drained,

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:26.790
you're exhausted or
you're tired, or you're bored.

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:29.428
Those are negative tasks.

00:21:29.428 --> 00:21:31.178
So take those things and
put on a chart, and

00:21:31.178 --> 00:21:32.890
I'll show you what the chart looks like.

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:36.330
And I just charted my week just
as example for you to use.

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:43.400
Here's my week, so to start out
the beginning of the t method.

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:47.790
That long tall thing that says flow
next to it should have art class,

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:49.670
somehow that got moves off the slide, but

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:53.050
it's over there art class is the first
thing I do on Monday nights.

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:54.771
It's a high energy activity.

00:21:54.771 --> 00:21:56.930
I have budgeting meetings,
I'm the executive director of our program.

00:21:56.930 --> 00:22:00.504
I have to have a budget meeting every
week, it's kind of a boring meeting,

00:22:00.504 --> 00:22:03.060
I feel a little bummed out when I'm done.

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:06.970
I love my office hours, those are lots
of fun, that's a high energy activity.

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:08.950
I love talking to my students.

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:10.520
The faculty team meeting is a funny one.

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:14.829
I sit with the faculty who are doing
the robotic cars here at Stanford,

00:22:14.829 --> 00:22:21.380
robotic medicine, and amazing K through 12
program at the D school, and other things.

00:22:21.380 --> 00:22:22.850
And when we're talking about that stuff,

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:27.120
the faculty meetings are like this
incredible intellectual salon.

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:31.550
And I love them, and then every once in a
while we'll have a 25 minute conversation

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:34.690
on who forgot to write down how many
copies they made on the copier.

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:37.870
And now the copier tally is out of order,
and

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:41.760
I'm thinking this is the dumbest
thing we could be talking about.

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:43.870
I've got the smartest people in
the world sitting around the table,

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:47.520
and I'm just like here's 20 bucks,
zero the tally and we'll start again.

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:49.920
I don't care, so
sometimes those are dumb meetings.

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:52.120
I love walking around the campus,
that's always fun for

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:53.550
my little bit of physical health.

00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:55.660
Teaching is fun,
I don't like house cleaning.

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:56.670
I love date night with my wife.

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:58.592
And this was the weird one,
master's coaching.

00:22:58.592 --> 00:23:01.807
I should like my master's students,
I admitted them, and

00:23:01.807 --> 00:23:05.940
they're my favorite students, and
I coach them on their thesis projects.

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:08.161
And that wasn't working very well, so

00:23:08.161 --> 00:23:11.740
you do your chart like that and
you're looking for two things.

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:13.110
You're looking for moments of flow.

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:15.810
And I've identified two office hours and
my art class.

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:19.350
My art class starts at 6:30,
we're doing figure drawings.

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:23.160
Sometimes by 9:30 I look up and I'm like,
why is the model coming off the stage?

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:26.949
Are we done?
What's going on here, this is so much fun.

00:23:26.949 --> 00:23:30.471
Thinking with working with my students,
I often go well over time in my office

00:23:30.471 --> 00:23:33.613
hours, because I just think
the conversation with the students is so

00:23:33.613 --> 00:23:36.850
interesting and their growth and
development is so interesting.

00:23:36.850 --> 00:23:39.450
And that's why I'm here,
children are amazing.

00:23:39.450 --> 00:23:43.511
Coaching was a little bit odd in
that it didn't really work for me.

00:23:43.511 --> 00:23:47.890
And so I came up with some
strategies to fix that.

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:50.660
And I'll show you those in a second.

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:53.280
But just one little side
bar on this energy thing.

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:58.060
If you really want to talk about
energy in true engineering terms.

00:23:58.060 --> 00:24:02.610
The human body runs on 2,000 calories
a day, 2,000 kilocalories a day of energy.

00:24:02.610 --> 00:24:04.290
That's how much food you eat, and

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:08.720
we convert it at some level of efficiency
into the energy that runs your body.

00:24:10.793 --> 00:24:14.861
So you would imagine that energy
is distributed over the body,

00:24:14.861 --> 00:24:19.030
sort of pro rata,
the size of the organs, but it's not.

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:20.680
The human brain, which is only about 2 or

00:24:20.680 --> 00:24:23.790
3% of the body,
consumes 500 calories a day.

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:29.310
It consumes 25% of all
the energy that you run on.

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:33.650
And so, what's really clear is that what,
and if it consumed any more than that,

00:24:33.650 --> 00:24:35.510
probably the rest of
the body couldn't function.

00:24:35.510 --> 00:24:40.174
So we assume evolutionarily it's evolve
to take as much energy as it can, but

00:24:40.174 --> 00:24:42.129
no more than will be in balance.

00:24:42.129 --> 00:24:46.198
But since it's so
disproportionate to the size and

00:24:46.198 --> 00:24:51.370
the weight of the organ itself,
it must be important, right?

00:24:51.370 --> 00:24:55.600
And so this idea that what we actually
spend our attention on what we attend to.

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:57.360
We're paying attention to this meeting.

00:24:57.360 --> 00:25:00.323
We're paying attention to something.

00:25:00.323 --> 00:25:02.450
We're paying attention to worrying.

00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:04.880
We're paying attention
to negative thoughts.

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:08.258
That is what the energy of
your brain gets spent on.

00:25:08.258 --> 00:25:13.076
And so you want to be very aware
of where are the positive negative

00:25:13.076 --> 00:25:15.500
loads on that attention.

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:18.825
Because that changes your
perception of how your day's going.

00:25:18.825 --> 00:25:22.160
And I'm not talking about
just thinking happy thoughts.

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:27.380
I'm just talking about being mindful
of what you pay attention to,

00:25:27.380 --> 00:25:30.740
what you talk about in your head,
and in the world.

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:35.190
Because that is truly how you
represent reality to you.

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:37.540
So in the engagement energy tool,

00:25:37.540 --> 00:25:41.160
you just notice what's consuming
lots of your energies.

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:44.652
What's generating more energies, or
what's generative in the energy space.

00:25:44.652 --> 00:25:49.830
And then that simple chart, you can use to
redistribute or redefine your engagements.

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:52.159
Like when I notice flow states,
I double down on those.

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:57.704
I'm actually going back to the studio
tonight to do some more drawing,

00:25:57.704 --> 00:25:59.400
because my wife's out of town.

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:04.010
And when I notice a negative thing,
I either fix it or understand it.

00:26:04.010 --> 00:26:07.530
So in the case of, for instance,
you notice I was, the budget meeting's

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:10.990
not that positive, but I'm the executive
director, I have to do budgets.

00:26:10.990 --> 00:26:11.745
I can't say I'm not going to do budgets.

00:26:11.745 --> 00:26:14.511
Sometimes actually the master
stroke is just say,

00:26:14.511 --> 00:26:18.190
I'm going to stop doing that task,
but that's not always possible.

00:26:18.190 --> 00:26:21.274
So what I do now is I
take my masters coaching,

00:26:21.274 --> 00:26:24.625
and I put it between office hours and
my workout.

00:26:24.625 --> 00:26:28.710
So I, excuse me, my budget meeting
being off status of workout.

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:31.910
And therefore by surrounding a low energy
thing with two higher energy things,

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:32.950
I negated it's influence.

00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:37.890
And at the end of the week, I feel very
positive that the week was useful.

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:42.340
And the masters coaching thing, I realized
I was doing the coaching in our studio.

00:26:42.340 --> 00:26:45.590
It's called the Loft,
it's a great place but it's very messy.

00:26:45.590 --> 00:26:49.430
And I couldn't get the focus and
attention I wanted in that place.

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:51.960
So I didn't want to move it to my office,
because then we're in

00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:55.100
the professor's office, and
the conversation won't be natural.

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:57.950
So I moved it to the terrace
outside the coffee house.

00:26:57.950 --> 00:27:02.440
And now I buy the students a cup of
coffee, and we have our engagements there.

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:04.270
And just by exchanging the place,

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:08.960
I totally changed the way the energy shows
up for me, and now it's a big positive.

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:11.530
I'm sure it's a much more positive
experience for the students as well,

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:14.510
because I'm more focused and
more attentive.

00:27:14.510 --> 00:27:17.010
So you can change place,
you can change sequence,

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:19.660
you can change a number
of different things.

00:27:19.660 --> 00:27:24.558
And in the book and in the class we
have a thing we call the AEIOU method.

00:27:24.558 --> 00:27:28.429
You can change activities,
you can change engagements,

00:27:28.429 --> 00:27:32.240
you can change locations,
that kind of a thing.

00:27:32.240 --> 00:27:34.870
So take a look at that, but
it's a really simple tool,

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:37.890
even just to bring to awareness what
it is you're paying attention to.

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:41.410
And then the last one I want to talk about
is this notion of a gravity problem.

00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:46.240
I'm sure you have a friend, not you, but
a friend who you've been going to coffee

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:48.900
with or lunch with for
the last couple years.

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:53.430
And they say things like my boss sucks,
or I don't like my partner,

00:27:53.430 --> 00:27:55.359
or my job's terrible, or something.

00:27:56.370 --> 00:28:00.093
They have a whole litany of problems, and
every time you get together with them,

00:28:00.093 --> 00:28:01.310
it's the same problems.

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:05.821
And so there's a class of problems in
the world we call gravity problems,

00:28:05.821 --> 00:28:09.920
because you can't actually solve them,
they're not solvable.

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:17.200
And when you run across a problem that's
not solvable, sort of continuing to act on

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:22.462
it, it just causes a huge sense
of disappointment and defeat.

00:28:22.462 --> 00:28:24.172
My co-author Dave would say,

00:28:24.172 --> 00:28:27.278
you can't solve a problem
you're not willing to have.

00:28:27.278 --> 00:28:30.782
So the first step in identifying
whether it's a gravity problem or

00:28:30.782 --> 00:28:34.610
not is really to identify, is this
a problem that's not even solvable?

00:28:34.610 --> 00:28:37.655
Because it's not actionable in any way,
it's just a circumstance, like gravity.

00:28:37.655 --> 00:28:42.470
Dave was dealing with someone who was
working in a family-run corporation.

00:28:42.470 --> 00:28:45.430
The name of the corporation was
the last name of the family.

00:28:45.430 --> 00:28:48.460
And he was complaining,
I'm a vice president of marketing,

00:28:48.460 --> 00:28:50.710
but I can never become the president
of the company because

00:28:51.770 --> 00:28:53.860
I'm not a member of the family and
my name's not on the door.

00:28:55.140 --> 00:28:56.844
And Dave said, you're absolutely right,

00:28:56.844 --> 00:28:58.880
you can never become
the president of this company.

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:00.880
Now what do you want to do about that?

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:03.810
Is that a problem you
want to try to solve, or

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:05.340
is that just something you
want to complain about?

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:08.340
Because if it's just complaining,
it's a gravity problem.

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:10.420
Now we're saying you
can't fight city hall.

00:29:10.420 --> 00:29:13.370
If you decide to fight city hall, you
decide to fight systemic racism, if you

00:29:13.370 --> 00:29:18.440
decide, as a woman, that your mission is
going to be to end sexism in the office.

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:21.570
Then it's no longer a gravity problem,
because it's a problem you're willing

00:29:21.570 --> 00:29:23.420
to take on,
a problem you're willing to solve.

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:28.176
But, if you just want to work, and
you just want to be happy, and

00:29:28.176 --> 00:29:32.814
you just want to get stuff done, the first
step in gravity problems is to accept.

00:29:32.814 --> 00:29:36.036
And I'm not suggesting that
you accept systemic racism or

00:29:36.036 --> 00:29:37.556
anything thing like that.

00:29:37.556 --> 00:29:41.330
You just have to, this is a system
that I have to deal with in the world.

00:29:41.330 --> 00:29:45.160
And now to be effective I have to
decide how I want to deal with it.

00:29:45.160 --> 00:29:46.485
Be careful of gravity problems,

00:29:46.485 --> 00:29:50.590
because we see people sucked into
these black holes over and over again.

00:29:50.590 --> 00:29:52.522
And it really keeps them
stuck in their lives.

00:29:52.522 --> 00:29:55.403
And the one thing we hear in
all the workshops is that,

00:29:55.403 --> 00:29:57.902
once you recognize what
a gravity problem is,

00:29:57.902 --> 00:30:02.020
you learn how to reframe problems to
work on them in a more effective manner.

00:30:02.020 --> 00:30:06.440
And you start looking at things
like the energy and engagement.

00:30:06.440 --> 00:30:12.319
You can really up the quality of
the experience of you week and

00:30:12.319 --> 00:30:19.128
of your job and of your life career
partnerships in a significant way.

00:30:19.128 --> 00:30:26.126
So I want to talk about, I'm sorry,
I meant to do the filter.

00:30:26.126 --> 00:30:32.910
The solution to gravity problems, of
course, is to start with the first step.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:36.960
And actually we add
accept in the diagram of

00:30:36.960 --> 00:30:41.280
design thinking with respect to your life,
we add the accept step first.

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:42.102
Look, it's really simple.

00:30:42.102 --> 00:30:46.653
When we sat down seven, eight,
ten years ago to design this, I said,

00:30:46.653 --> 00:30:51.358
I think design thinking can be applied
to the problem of an individual and

00:30:51.358 --> 00:30:55.162
a life, rather than the problem
of designing a new phone.

00:30:55.162 --> 00:30:56.983
And so
we went looking to see if that were true,

00:30:56.983 --> 00:30:58.780
because I didn't want
to force the analogy.

00:31:00.730 --> 00:31:03.590
So in this case,
well first of all you start with accept.

00:31:03.590 --> 00:31:07.740
Am I working on something
I'm willing to work on?

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:11.260
Is there something in my life, or

00:31:11.260 --> 00:31:15.210
is there a need in my life that
I need to satisfy or solve?

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:19.270
When we teach this idea of need finding,
of using empathy to go out and

00:31:19.270 --> 00:31:27.040
understand what people need, we say
a need is a gap between what you want and

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:30.980
the use or usability or the meaning of
the thing that you're trying to get to.

00:31:30.980 --> 00:31:32.470
So there's some kind of a gap.

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:34.334
So we noticed with students and

00:31:34.334 --> 00:31:39.446
with other people who are thinking about
their lives, the gap was this meaning gap.

00:31:39.446 --> 00:31:44.550
I'm doing stuff, I'm working, I have jobs,
but I don't know what it heads adds to.

00:31:44.550 --> 00:31:48.427
And there's no structure in the world
that's going to tell me that,

00:31:48.427 --> 00:31:51.001
I'm going to have to figure it out for
myself.

00:31:51.001 --> 00:31:54.320
So all the original ethnography we did
on this problem with our students, and

00:31:54.320 --> 00:31:58.790
with mid-career people, and with encore
career people, people who are retiring.

00:31:58.790 --> 00:32:01.260
And they wanted, but
people are retiring earlier now and

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:03.100
they're certainly retiring healthier now.

00:32:03.100 --> 00:32:07.970
So they've got ten or 20 years of
productive life in them after they retire.

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:11.700
And they go, well I want to
do something with this stuff,

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:14.450
but I don't know how to get to
the thing that's meaningful.

00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:16.850
The thing that gives me
some kind of purpose.

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:21.110
The reason I get up on Monday morning and
say, I'm really looking forward to going

00:32:21.110 --> 00:32:24.820
into work, rather than, my god, do I have
to spend another week during that stuff?

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:30.540
So the idea that we could turn empathy on
ourselves, so empathy for our own gaps,

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:35.550
for our own spaces between what
we want and this idea of meaning.

00:32:35.550 --> 00:32:37.780
And that we could also turn
our empathy on the world,

00:32:37.780 --> 00:32:40.740
that what does the world need from you,
me?

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:46.120
They will say when your greatest
gift is given to the world.

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:47.750
In a way the world really wants it.

00:32:47.750 --> 00:32:49.650
Then you have a perfect match right.

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:53.270
Just because I'm passionate about
something doesn't mean the world

00:32:53.270 --> 00:32:54.600
wants to pay me for it.

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:58.520
Just because I'm excited about something
doesn't mean that the world is excited

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:00.030
about what I want.

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:04.440
One of the things we try to see is,
would it be possible to use empathy for

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:08.240
ourselves and empathy for
the world to close this gap on meaning.

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:12.370
To do that we realize, well,
there's these gravity problems.

00:33:12.370 --> 00:33:14.160
There's these things
that keep people stopped.

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:17.370
We're going to have to reframe all of
this stuff, but that's perfect for

00:33:17.370 --> 00:33:20.000
the define part of the problem.

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:22.580
And then we know that if you have lots and
lots of ideas,

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:25.250
you're going to have better
things to choose from.

00:33:25.250 --> 00:33:29.435
There's tons and tons of data, so we
evolve these ideas there's not one of you,

00:33:29.435 --> 00:33:30.425
there's at least three.

00:33:30.425 --> 00:33:34.695
We're going to ideate on three
versions of parallel network one.

00:33:34.695 --> 00:33:37.885
We're going to ideate and
mind map and brainstorm and

00:33:37.885 --> 00:33:40.485
do those well,
teach you the way designers do those so

00:33:40.485 --> 00:33:44.955
they're highly productive and
outcome driven activities.

00:33:44.955 --> 00:33:48.045
Not just we had a lot of ideas now and
I don't know what to do with them.

00:33:48.045 --> 00:33:51.705
So we knew we could do that and
then fundamentally since you're trying to

00:33:51.705 --> 00:33:56.540
create the future of you and
the future is unknowable.

00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:59.880
You don't know if the thing you
want to do will be successful.

00:33:59.880 --> 00:34:01.770
You don't know if you even
really want to do it.

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:05.360
And this notion that you had to
pick something go all in and

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:08.600
if it didn't work well you
didn't get the best outcome

00:34:08.600 --> 00:34:11.310
just seemed like that's not the way
designers approach the problem.

00:34:11.310 --> 00:34:16.720
Now I was at Apple a long time ago
when we went to the first laptops.

00:34:16.720 --> 00:34:21.107
So, I wasn't there when they were doing
the phone, but, if you read about

00:34:21.107 --> 00:34:25.982
Steve Jobs' biography by Walter Isaacson,
you read about the story of the phone,

00:34:25.982 --> 00:34:28.908
they prototyped the phone
hundreds of times, and

00:34:28.908 --> 00:34:31.089
they showed it to Steve three times.

00:34:31.089 --> 00:34:35.490
Twice he turned it down and
the third time he said it was good enough.

00:34:35.490 --> 00:34:38.320
This notion that we're going to
work on one singular version,

00:34:38.320 --> 00:34:39.790
is just not the way designers work.

00:34:39.790 --> 00:34:43.860
They're working on multiple ideas for the
interaction, the interface, the screen,

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:47.368
the original one didn't
have a finger-print sensor.

00:34:47.368 --> 00:34:49.440
There's all the things
that they put in there,

00:34:49.440 --> 00:34:53.280
they had no idea what the outcome
would be when they started.

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:57.330
They just knew that they wanted something
in Steve's parlance that would be

00:34:57.330 --> 00:35:02.100
insanely great and
would reinvent the category of phones.

00:35:02.100 --> 00:35:04.980
And so
the willingness to sort of build and

00:35:04.980 --> 00:35:10.920
test your way forward is what's
the core principle in design thinking.

00:35:10.920 --> 00:35:13.860
And then when we parted that over to
designing your life it just makes

00:35:13.860 --> 00:35:14.780
perfect sense.

00:35:14.780 --> 00:35:16.100
What's an information interview?

00:35:16.100 --> 00:35:18.900
It's a prototype of you talking to someone

00:35:18.900 --> 00:35:21.290
who might be doing something that
you are interested in doing.

00:35:21.290 --> 00:35:25.280
They're actually you and your future,
they've been doing it for years.

00:35:25.280 --> 00:35:29.270
You've been just thinking about maybe that
might be something I'm interested in.

00:35:29.270 --> 00:35:31.480
Having what we call a prototype
interview with someone and

00:35:31.480 --> 00:35:35.580
doing it well to get their story,
should leave some resonance in you.

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:39.730
You'll hear a story that either
rings a bell in your heart or

00:35:39.730 --> 00:35:40.580
your mind, or doesn't.

00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:43.580
And that's a great piece of
information about whether that teacher

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:44.630
would work for you.

00:35:44.630 --> 00:35:48.720
A prototype experience going to shadow
somebody for a day during a one-week kind

00:35:48.720 --> 00:35:54.000
of internship working on a project
together is a great way to discover

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:59.310
whether that career path or that activity
set is something that your interested in.

00:35:59.310 --> 00:36:02.120
So this notion of building
your way forward David Kelley

00:36:02.120 --> 00:36:03.950
says we build to think.

00:36:03.950 --> 00:36:06.820
We make something so
that we promote the world instead.

00:36:06.820 --> 00:36:10.150
This is the possible future what do you
think, and everybody talks about it.

00:36:10.150 --> 00:36:14.450
And then we get new ideas Is a wonderful
way to protect your life, and

00:36:14.450 --> 00:36:19.560
it avoids the possibility that
you'll go all in on something,

00:36:19.560 --> 00:36:24.440
discover it's not what you thought it
would be, and then be disappointed and

00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:31.490
have to pivot or reset, and that can be
pretty costly if you're far down the path.

00:36:31.490 --> 00:36:33.270
It's been wonderful to go out and

00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:36.310
do workshops on this stuff with
the Stanford Alumni Association.

00:36:36.310 --> 00:36:39.850
I get to meet tons of really wonderful
alums and they all have wonderful stories.

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:45.720
Some of the cautionary stories I hear are,
and it's kind of paradoxical.

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:50.160
Wow, I'm super successful,
I am a partner at the firm.

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:54.230
Pick any firm, law firm,
business consulting firm, whatever.

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:56.910
I'm making lots of money, seven figures.

00:36:58.900 --> 00:37:06.280
I'm able to support a fantastic lifestyle
for my family and I'm miserable.

00:37:06.280 --> 00:37:07.660
And one woman said to me,

00:37:07.660 --> 00:37:10.789
it steals a little piece of my
soul every day to go to work.

00:37:11.800 --> 00:37:14.142
And I said well that can't be,
that doesn't sound healthy.

00:37:14.142 --> 00:37:19.630
[LAUGH] Let's work on that, and
she said no, you don't understand.

00:37:19.630 --> 00:37:25.200
I'm stuck, I'm the youngest woman at the
firm, the only woman partner at the firm.

00:37:25.200 --> 00:37:29.170
I need to do this to uphold this image,

00:37:29.170 --> 00:37:34.090
I need to do this because I believe in it,
but I hate the work I'm doing.

00:37:34.090 --> 00:37:39.310
And we built the white cell that requires
this kind of money and everything else.

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:44.630
And so, I'm meeting people in other words
that haven't had a chance to stop on this.

00:37:44.630 --> 00:37:47.310
They are very successful people obviously.

00:37:47.310 --> 00:37:53.760
But success distract them into a situation
and a lifestyle that they never wanted.

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:55.310
They never thought about it very much,

00:37:55.310 --> 00:37:58.800
they just sort of went for
the next great shiny accomplishment.

00:37:58.800 --> 00:38:04.300
Because they're smart and people full of
capacity, they were successful in getting

00:38:04.300 --> 00:38:07.410
the things they never asked
themselves do I really want this?

00:38:08.620 --> 00:38:13.280
And so the earlier you can start engaging
in this design process and saying hey,

00:38:13.280 --> 00:38:17.340
before I decide that being a partner
at the law firm is what I really want,

00:38:17.340 --> 00:38:20.870
I probably ought to go shadow a partner.

00:38:20.870 --> 00:38:23.240
Maybe have a conversation
with a couple of associates,

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:25.920
take a few people out to lunch and dinner.

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:29.010
Somebody must know somebody who
can introduce me to someone

00:38:29.010 --> 00:38:30.500
at these situations.

00:38:30.500 --> 00:38:35.360
Or do I want to be a professor, do I
want to be, you know all these things.

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:39.820
There's a famous science fiction writer
William Gibson who I love and he

00:38:39.820 --> 00:38:44.730
has a famous quote, our future is already
here, it's just unevenly distributed.

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:47.380
Somebody if you can't know anything
about your personal future.

00:38:47.380 --> 00:38:50.670
But someone is probably living
a very similar analogous future, and

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:54.380
they're already doing it,
they've been doing it for years, and so

00:38:54.380 --> 00:38:56.600
they represent a little
piece of you in the future.

00:38:56.600 --> 00:39:00.060
The ability to learn to prototype
your way into these experiences,

00:39:00.060 --> 00:39:03.690
try things in a really
low threat situation.

00:39:03.690 --> 00:39:06.680
Dave and I say, set the bar low,
clear it, do it again.

00:39:06.680 --> 00:39:12.148
Design these information interviews,
these information prototypes And

00:39:12.148 --> 00:39:15.099
information prototypic experiences,

00:39:15.099 --> 00:39:19.280
to learn how to imagine your future,
it's so critical.

00:39:19.280 --> 00:39:22.546
And the other thing that's true,
in product design,

00:39:22.546 --> 00:39:25.320
I'll do a bunch of ethnography and
research.

00:39:25.320 --> 00:39:28.600
And I'll come back with a product that
everybody, in the group I'm working with,

00:39:28.600 --> 00:39:31.280
said they wanted, and
then I'll show them the new prototype.

00:39:31.280 --> 00:39:35.510
And then they'll say now that we see that,
I've changed my mind,

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:36.510
that's not what I want anymore.

00:39:37.590 --> 00:39:40.470
And it's very frustrating
[LAUGH] as a designer or

00:39:40.470 --> 00:39:43.450
an engineer to hear that the customer
keeps changing their mind.

00:39:43.450 --> 00:39:46.120
But what happened in the moment when I
showed them the thing that they said

00:39:46.120 --> 00:39:46.660
that they wanted.

00:39:46.660 --> 00:39:51.770
When they actually realized what
is possible in this new future,

00:39:51.770 --> 00:39:52.850
of course they changed their mind.

00:39:52.850 --> 00:39:56.707
Because their needs changed, because now
they know something It's possible that

00:39:56.707 --> 00:40:00.822
they could have not imagined and
that's exactly what we want to happen,

00:40:00.822 --> 00:40:05.138
in fact it's not frustrating is this
notion of what I got in the world I engage

00:40:05.138 --> 00:40:09.473
the world in radical collaboration
with curiosity to designers mindsets.

00:40:09.473 --> 00:40:13.600
I've reframed the problem and I'm looking
for people who are living in my future.

00:40:13.600 --> 00:40:15.932
And I'm talking to them and
I'm engaging them, and

00:40:15.932 --> 00:40:19.330
I'm even prototyping little versions
of what it would be like if I did that.

00:40:20.530 --> 00:40:23.010
And I'm having the embodied
experience of that, and

00:40:23.010 --> 00:40:27.150
I'm having the physical experience and
the intellectual experience of that.

00:40:27.150 --> 00:40:28.910
Something Dave and
I call narrative resonance.

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:30.260
Is that story my story?

00:40:30.260 --> 00:40:31.990
Do I hear myself in that one?

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:35.050
When two tuning forks are in the same
room and they're on the same pitch.

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:38.010
You ring one,
the other one rings in sympathy.

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:42.889
So do I have a sympathetic resonance
with the future that I'm exploring?

00:40:42.889 --> 00:40:43.981
And if I do, and

00:40:43.981 --> 00:40:49.283
if I find myself in states of flow every
once in a while as I work into this, or

00:40:49.283 --> 00:40:54.522
prototype, or build my way forward,
then I know I'm on the right track.

00:40:54.522 --> 00:40:57.077
Anyway I want to waste some time for
questions, so

00:40:57.077 --> 00:40:59.130
let me just kind of hit the takeaways.

00:40:59.130 --> 00:41:02.575
I believe we have demonstrated through
our research and through the 3 or

00:41:02.575 --> 00:41:07.210
4,000 students, on campus and off,
that you can in fact design your life.

00:41:07.210 --> 00:41:09.480
And kind of you have to,
because if you don't design it,

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:12.170
it's going to get designed for
you in some ad hoc process.

00:41:12.170 --> 00:41:15.290
And then you're just going to
be responding to life,

00:41:15.290 --> 00:41:18.660
rather than trying to sort of manage and
way find it.

00:41:18.660 --> 00:41:23.040
For most people, passion's a poor starting
point, because 80% don't have one.

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:28.510
Again if you have one, awesome, go for
it, that will be an organizing principle,

00:41:28.510 --> 00:41:32.050
but you will still prototype interview and
prototype experience your way forward,

00:41:32.050 --> 00:41:35.180
because you still don't know your
future any better than we do.

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:37.960
A flow journal is one way of noticing.

00:41:37.960 --> 00:41:41.830
Flowing energy engagements
are really important things

00:41:41.830 --> 00:41:44.270
to know since not the time
you spend on something.

00:41:44.270 --> 00:41:46.500
It's the energy you spend on something.

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:52.760
Our attention which comes from this funny
three pounds of gooey stuff in our brain.

00:41:52.760 --> 00:41:56.340
Our attention is what consumes our energy,
we spend our time and

00:41:56.340 --> 00:41:59.110
attention on the things we
are talking to ourselves about.

00:41:59.110 --> 00:42:04.420
So be very, very aware of what you're
spending your time and attention on.

00:42:04.420 --> 00:42:09.230
Dave has a phrase and
it's a little bit, maybe, trite.

00:42:09.230 --> 00:42:13.090
But he says, if you don't like
your reality, change your mind.

00:42:13.090 --> 00:42:15.450
Actually, the phrase
comes from his mentor.

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:18.110
It's not as easy as that,

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:22.950
obviously when stuff will happen in
reality we like to say biased action,

00:42:22.950 --> 00:42:26.590
because that's the designer's mindset
rather than bias to planning.

00:42:26.590 --> 00:42:29.840
Planning is great, but
there's an old military expression,

00:42:29.840 --> 00:42:32.030
no plan of battle survives
first contact with the enemy.

00:42:32.030 --> 00:42:35.783
And I would argue no plan for your life
is going to survive contact with reality.

00:42:35.783 --> 00:42:40.770
Stuff will happen in reality;
you will have to deal with it.

00:42:40.770 --> 00:42:44.728
Things you will find, bad things will
happen, disappointing things will happen.

00:42:44.728 --> 00:42:48.657
Opportunities that you want and
will not be available to you, so

00:42:48.657 --> 00:42:51.660
you will have to re-plan
with action in real time.

00:42:52.810 --> 00:42:57.250
But what you pay attention to and how you
frame those experiences, the positive and

00:42:57.250 --> 00:43:01.690
the negative ones is how you will
experience meaning in your life.

00:43:01.690 --> 00:43:04.690
It's not a zero sum game and
that you're never too late.

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:10.010
You can reset this counter
at any point and start over.

00:43:10.010 --> 00:43:15.560
&gt;&gt; With that I'd like to take
a few minutes as time permits to

00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:19.710
ask Bill a few questions that have come
up and we invite you to submit additional

00:43:19.710 --> 00:43:24.190
questions we'll get to as many as we
can in the 10 minutes that we have.

00:43:24.190 --> 00:43:29.190
So first of all, Bill, one note that
we got from the audience which I

00:43:29.190 --> 00:43:33.000
liked is someone said that this last
hour has been a state of flow for them.

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:34.274
So-
&gt;&gt; So that's Great.

00:43:34.274 --> 00:43:36.230
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was glad to hear that.

00:43:36.230 --> 00:43:41.240
So I think one question that
came up earlier in the webinar,

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:44.480
which I think is important to address and
kind of an elephant in the room,

00:43:44.480 --> 00:43:47.340
potentially in as you think about careers.

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:50.420
What is the role of money in this scheme?

00:43:50.420 --> 00:43:51.370
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:43:51.370 --> 00:43:55.730
This is one of the most well
researched topics in psychology and

00:43:55.730 --> 00:43:57.460
positive psychology particularly.

00:43:57.460 --> 00:43:58.740
Now does money make you happy?

00:43:58.740 --> 00:44:01.775
How much money do you have to
have in order to be happy?

00:44:01.775 --> 00:44:04.140
The pursuit of money,

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:10.200
which also includes money gives you
capacity to do different things.

00:44:10.200 --> 00:44:11.140
Is that important?

00:44:11.140 --> 00:44:12.850
Is that what makes you happy?

00:44:12.850 --> 00:44:17.641
And particularly for people who are
knowledge workers, folks that are working

00:44:17.641 --> 00:44:22.501
not on an assembly line where you get paid
by the number of parts you assemble, but

00:44:22.501 --> 00:44:27.436
doing work that's cognitive work or some
other kind of work The research is very,

00:44:27.436 --> 00:44:28.249
very clear.

00:44:28.249 --> 00:44:33.740
Once you have enough money, and enough
mean you're not worried about your bills.

00:44:33.740 --> 00:44:37.550
You're not worried about your future.

00:44:37.550 --> 00:44:43.630
You can save enough money to feel
like you'll have what you need.

00:44:43.630 --> 00:44:47.510
Once you have enough,
incremental amounts after that

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:55.550
lead to absolutely no more happiness or
sense of purpose in your life.

00:44:55.550 --> 00:44:57.250
So, if you're Warren Buffet,

00:44:57.250 --> 00:45:01.110
it doesn't matter, after you've got
a couple hundred thousand dollars and

00:45:01.110 --> 00:45:04.380
you can live in the house you want to
live and you can do everything else.

00:45:04.380 --> 00:45:05.120
Having a yacht and

00:45:05.120 --> 00:45:08.289
everything else does not increase
Warren Buffet' happiness at all.

00:45:09.290 --> 00:45:11.530
So this notion that you've
got to pursue money for

00:45:11.530 --> 00:45:13.670
happiness is a really toxic notion.

00:45:13.670 --> 00:45:18.226
Now if you don't have enough or if you're
insecure that the flow of money might be

00:45:18.226 --> 00:45:22.257
interrupted, because your industry
is in turmoil or something else.

00:45:22.257 --> 00:45:26.720
Those are all real problems,
and those you can work on.

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:29.760
But there's a thing,
psychologist called the hedonic treadmill.

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:31.440
That hedonism is the search of pleasure,
right?

00:45:31.440 --> 00:45:33.540
And the hedonic treadmill is
just a treadmill of pleasure.

00:45:33.540 --> 00:45:38.590
So I would go to work and I'm happy for
a while, then I get bored.

00:45:38.590 --> 00:45:42.510
So I get a raise and a promotion,
now I'm happy again.

00:45:42.510 --> 00:45:44.630
And then roughly about
6 to 8 months later,

00:45:44.630 --> 00:45:49.940
I'm back to the exact same state of
whatever my rest state was and I'm bored.

00:45:49.940 --> 00:45:52.766
So my conclusion of course, is well it was
nice to get a raise but it wasn't enough.

00:45:52.766 --> 00:45:56.730
If I could have more, then I'd be happier.

00:45:56.730 --> 00:46:00.580
So then I get another raise,
I get another promotion.

00:46:00.580 --> 00:46:02.940
I end up partner at the law firm.

00:46:02.940 --> 00:46:04.272
I make $650,000 base
with a $2 million bonus.

00:46:04.272 --> 00:46:08.505
And I'm no happier than I was
than when I was the associate and

00:46:08.505 --> 00:46:11.260
I'm making $150,000 a year.

00:46:11.260 --> 00:46:12.616
In fact I'm more miserable,

00:46:12.616 --> 00:46:15.393
because I have more constraints
on my behaviors and my time.

00:46:15.393 --> 00:46:20.051
So it's absolutely clear money does not
make you happy, you of course have to be

00:46:20.051 --> 00:46:24.590
in a position where your safety,
security and basic needs are met.

00:46:24.590 --> 00:46:31.579
Once that has occurred, take money
off the table and go for purpose.

00:46:31.579 --> 00:46:36.600
Dan Pink's book, Dan Pink is
a fantastic guy that we worked with.

00:46:36.600 --> 00:46:38.638
He's written a bunch of different books.

00:46:38.638 --> 00:46:43.330
But in the book Drive, where he talks
about what motivates people to work hard,

00:46:43.330 --> 00:46:46.330
All the research says it's autonomy,
mastery, and purpose, you have to have

00:46:46.330 --> 00:46:49.620
purpose for your work, mastery means
you're learning all the time, and

00:46:49.620 --> 00:46:54.010
autonomy means you're deciding how to get
the tasks done that you want to get done.

00:46:54.010 --> 00:46:54.640
Doesn't mean you work for

00:46:54.640 --> 00:46:57.600
yourself, doesn't mean you
have nothing micro managed.

00:46:57.600 --> 00:47:02.860
If you have those three things,
money will not make you happy,

00:47:02.860 --> 00:47:05.390
search for things that actually do.

00:47:05.390 --> 00:47:08.775
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there's another comment that came
in from one of the listeners that said

00:47:08.775 --> 00:47:11.165
there's two amounts of money,
enough and not enough.

00:47:11.165 --> 00:47:15.575
So I think that's a nice
summary of that notion.

00:47:15.575 --> 00:47:18.940
&gt;&gt; By the way, people who win the lottery,
who win millions and millions of dollars

00:47:18.940 --> 00:47:21.861
within 18 months go back to the exact
same amount of satisfaction and

00:47:21.861 --> 00:47:23.335
happiness in their life.

00:47:23.335 --> 00:47:25.220
It makes no difference at all.

00:47:25.220 --> 00:47:27.710
If they were a miserable person,
they remain miserable.

00:47:27.710 --> 00:47:30.110
If they were happy person,
they remain happy.

00:47:30.110 --> 00:47:33.270
Wherever your set point is,
money doesn't change it.

00:47:33.270 --> 00:47:36.400
&gt;&gt; All right, so we're getting a lot
of more questions but we'll pick one.

00:47:36.400 --> 00:47:39.970
I don't think we'll have time for
more than one question.

00:47:39.970 --> 00:47:41.580
In the design thinking process,

00:47:41.580 --> 00:47:45.730
there is a lot of emphasis put on
getting feedback and testing with users,

00:47:45.730 --> 00:47:49.840
testing your prototype,
how do you do that in this situation?

00:47:49.840 --> 00:47:53.310
You've prototyped a number of live or
a number of activities.

00:47:53.310 --> 00:47:54.719
And how do you get feedback|?

00:47:54.719 --> 00:47:58.440
And maybe related to that, what are some
metrics that you use to evaluate the?

00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:02.711
[INAUDIBLE] Difference.

00:48:02.711 --> 00:48:04.231
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so quickly the feedback
method is again empathy for yourself.

00:48:04.231 --> 00:48:05.231
How did this feel to me?

00:48:05.231 --> 00:48:06.331
I did this little pro shop.

00:48:06.331 --> 00:48:07.565
I shadowed this doctor for a day.

00:48:07.565 --> 00:48:12.570
Or I went and I did a one-week
unpaid project with this group.

00:48:12.570 --> 00:48:13.848
How did I feel about that?

00:48:13.848 --> 00:48:15.288
What was my internal state?

00:48:15.288 --> 00:48:19.583
Both my emotional state and
my intellectual state, that's one measure.

00:48:19.583 --> 00:48:22.000
And then two, how was my work received?

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:23.950
It's unlikely that you're
going to be happy and

00:48:23.950 --> 00:48:30.060
feel like you're thriving if you
are not excellent at the things you do.

00:48:30.060 --> 00:48:32.630
You have your own strengths and
weaknesses, we all do.

00:48:32.630 --> 00:48:34.340
If I'm working from my strength and

00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:37.480
if the work product that I
deliver is well accepted.

00:48:37.480 --> 00:48:39.740
Well, that's exceptional we
really like working with you.

00:48:39.740 --> 00:48:41.320
Then I'm getting two kinds of feedback.

00:48:41.320 --> 00:48:45.730
I enjoy the work, and the people enjoy
my output that's in the work domain.

00:48:45.730 --> 00:48:47.860
But it would include any other domain.

00:48:47.860 --> 00:48:50.740
I mean you're unlikely to be happy
if you're not good at something.

00:48:50.740 --> 00:48:52.083
So you're looking for

00:48:52.083 --> 00:48:56.380
where in the world will the things
that I'm strong at be well received?

00:48:56.380 --> 00:48:58.823
And if I'm looking for money for
those things it's in the marketplace.

00:48:58.823 --> 00:49:03.841
If I'm in the world of art and
design then it's about having people

00:49:03.841 --> 00:49:09.140
who appreciate the quality of my art or
my work in that domain.

00:49:09.140 --> 00:49:12.140
So I need the feedback loop
from the world to say yeah,

00:49:12.140 --> 00:49:14.760
the thing that you're doing
that you're really good at?

00:49:14.760 --> 00:49:15.980
We want that too.

00:49:15.980 --> 00:49:18.110
There's an acceptance on the other side.

00:49:18.110 --> 00:49:22.165
Otherwise, you're the unpaid
artist in poverty.

00:49:22.165 --> 00:49:24.006
That's fine,
you can choose to be that person.

00:49:24.006 --> 00:49:25.374
There's nothing wrong with that.

00:49:25.374 --> 00:49:28.720
But you just have to accept that
the world doesn't want your art.

00:49:28.720 --> 00:49:32.390
Most artists don't care about
that problem, so that's okay.

00:49:32.390 --> 00:49:35.030
But if you're planning on this
being something that supports you,

00:49:35.030 --> 00:49:37.890
and remember there's your vocation and
your avocation.

00:49:37.890 --> 00:49:41.300
Vocation is what you do for money,
avocation is what you do for meaning.

00:49:41.300 --> 00:49:44.640
You may decide to make those the same
thing, that's a kind of modern idea.

00:49:44.640 --> 00:49:46.150
My grandfather, who came over from Wales,

00:49:46.150 --> 00:49:51.560
worked at the National Biscuit Company,
Nabisco, making cookies for 40 years.

00:49:51.560 --> 00:49:55.230
In the union, working hard, came home,
that was just making money.

00:49:55.230 --> 00:49:57.228
The meaning in his life was his family.

00:49:57.228 --> 00:50:00.780
He was a member of the Elks,
he was a member of his church.

00:50:00.780 --> 00:50:03.052
That's what drove the meaning in his life.

00:50:03.052 --> 00:50:06.173
So this notion that you're going to get it
all in one place is a very modern idea.

00:50:06.173 --> 00:50:10.173
But in any case, pick the place where
you want to get the feedback from and

00:50:10.173 --> 00:50:11.580
then listen carefully.

00:50:11.580 --> 00:50:14.200
Is the world responding
to things I'm offering?

00:50:15.590 --> 00:50:17.005
What was the second part?

00:50:17.005 --> 00:50:17.570
&gt;&gt; Metrics.

00:50:17.570 --> 00:50:20.670
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so the metrics would
be I'm doing my float journal,

00:50:20.670 --> 00:50:23.398
I'm noticing flow state
are popping up more often.

00:50:23.398 --> 00:50:25.450
I'm doing my energy mapping and

00:50:25.450 --> 00:50:30.870
I'm noticing that I'm leaving most weeks
with a fairly high energy reserve.

00:50:30.870 --> 00:50:33.950
And I'm excited and
enthusiastic about the next thing.

00:50:33.950 --> 00:50:38.686
And the other metric is, in the vocation
thing, is the world paying me what me what

00:50:38.686 --> 00:50:42.135
I think I make in order for
this to be meaningful work for me?

00:50:42.135 --> 00:50:44.536
So as long as I'm getting paid enough.

00:50:44.536 --> 00:50:49.003
You know [LAUGH] we do run into,
at these workshops, I'm a CEO.

00:50:49.003 --> 00:50:51.930
I make a lot of money, but
I really want to be a poet.

00:50:51.930 --> 00:50:52.840
I go great!

00:50:52.840 --> 00:50:56.460
As far as I know, there is nothing
stopping you from being a poet.

00:50:56.460 --> 00:50:57.654
No no no, you don't understand.

00:50:57.654 --> 00:50:59.870
I want to be a poet, and
I still need to make seven figures,

00:50:59.870 --> 00:51:01.840
because I got the house and
Teslas and blah blah blah.

00:51:01.840 --> 00:51:07.640
And I'm like okay, a, a gravity problem,
or b, maybe you should try rap.

00:51:07.640 --> 00:51:09.960
Maybe you're the next Dr. Dre.

00:51:09.960 --> 00:51:13.298
You don't look like it to me,
frankly [LAUGH] but

00:51:13.298 --> 00:51:17.062
the only seven-figure poets
I know write rap music.

00:51:17.062 --> 00:51:20.707
So if you're not willing to do
poetry on the market's terms,

00:51:20.707 --> 00:51:23.460
then my suggestion is to
keep your CEO day job.

00:51:23.460 --> 00:51:26.710
And go out to poetry slams at night,
and open mic nights at night.

00:51:26.710 --> 00:51:31.703
And get your avocation to fulfill
that part of you that wants to

00:51:31.703 --> 00:51:33.631
speak in poetic terms.

00:51:33.631 --> 00:51:37.537
But don't blame the world that there
are no seven figure salaries for

00:51:37.537 --> 00:51:39.840
poets because that's just gravity, dude.

00:51:42.390 --> 00:51:45.830
&gt;&gt; Great, wonderful, so
this was very informative.

00:51:45.830 --> 00:51:48.580
As I said, we had lots and
lots of questions and interests.

00:51:48.580 --> 00:51:53.143
All of you will be receiving a recording
of this webinar within a week so

00:51:53.143 --> 00:51:55.639
that you can review it again.

00:51:55.639 --> 00:51:57.479
And try and practice some of these things.

00:51:57.479 --> 00:51:58.797
And as we mentioned,

00:51:58.797 --> 00:52:03.201
we would love to see as many of you as
are interested here with us in June.

00:52:03.201 --> 00:52:06.583
To try and get some additional
practice with the design thinking and

00:52:06.583 --> 00:52:09.030
designing your life activities.

00:52:09.030 --> 00:52:11.939
Have a very good rest of your day and
thank you for joining us.

